    *   *Unreimbursed Employee Expenses:* $1,050. Under current tax law (the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act), W-2 employees can no longer deduct unreimbursed employee expenses. 
    *   *Total Itemized:* $950.
*   **Verdict:** You should absolutely take the **Standard Deduction ($14,600)**. It is significantly higher than your itemized deductions.

### 2. Student Loan Interest (Above-the-Line Deduction)
You paid $5,100 in student loan interest, but the IRS caps this deduction at **$2,500** per year. 
*   Because this is an "above-the-line" deduction (an adjustment to income), you get to claim this $2,500 *in addition* to your $14,600 standard deduction. 
*   *Note:* This deduction begins to phase out for single filers with a Modified Adjusted Gross Income (MAGI) over $80,000 in 2024. Depending on your final net business income, your MAGI will be hovering right around this threshold, so your $2,500 deduction might be slightly reduced.

### 3. Home Office Treatment (For Freelance Income)
Because you have 1099-NEC freelance income, you can deduct your home office against your self-employment income. You have two ways to calculate this:
*   **Simplified Method:** 170 sq ft × $5/sq ft = **$850 deduction**.
*   **Actual Expenses Method:** Your office is 170 sq ft out of a 740 sq ft apartment, meaning your business use percentage is **22.97%**. 
    *   Your annual rent is $22,560 ($1,880 × 12). 
